. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H3 * (Please adapt according to your needs)
. 
.         /*
>         To test this hypothesis, a dataset has been structured as a longitudi
> nal panel at the household level, with repeated observations for the same hou
> sehold 
>         across different survey waves (from 2000 to 2020). A household is def
> ined as a unique and stable pairing of individuals $i$ and $j$. That is, for 
> a given 
>         household, the composition in terms of individuals $i$ and $j$ remain
> s constant over time, although any given individual may belong to different h
> ouseholds
>         across waves (never more than one in a given wave).
>         
>         For consistency and interpretability, household $h$ is defined as com
> prising a woman $i$ and a man $j$. While the gender composition does not affe
> ct the symmetry 
>         of the equality index, this allows us to consistently control for pot
> ential gender differences in the effects of personal characteristics (e.g., e
> ducation) on 
>         the variable of interest.
>         
>         In line with this data structure, a fixed-effects regression model wi
> ll be employed to account for unobserved time-invariant household characteris
> tics that may be 
>         correlated with gender equality.
>         */
. 
.         xtset hid syear

Panel variable: hid (unbalanced)
 Time variable: syear, 2010 to 2020, but with gaps
         Delta: 1 unit

.         xtreg equality has_daughters_h has_kids_w age_w i.pgisced11_w i.pgfam
> std_w i.pgemplst_w has_kids_m age_m i.pgisced11_m i.pgfamstd_m i.pgemplst_m h
